@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security] host-only-vsock-audience
Every accepted connection MUST have vsock peer CID 2 and present a valid HMAC
proof bound to this boot's challenge, placement and execution epoch before any
control or attachment operation. Attach tokens are 128-bit random values scoped
to one session.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security] no-terminal-bytes-in-state-or-logs
Terminal bytes MUST NOT appear in logs, error strings, or any structure other
than a session ring buffer or an in-flight socket write. Titles and sequences
observed in output are guest data and share this rule.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:recovery] fidelity-passthrough
The live path MUST NOT drop, reorder, sanitize, or interpret terminal bytes.
Memory bounds are enforced exclusively through per-viewer high-water marks that
pause the PTY pump, never through truncation of live output. Scrollback rings
are raw bytes; a reattach older than retained history replays from the ring
start and reports the skipped cursor explicitly.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:recovery] sessions-outlive-connections
Sessions persist until an explicit kill, child exit, daemon stop, or idle
deadline. Viewer disconnects and dial errors MUST NOT terminate sessions.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security] guest-execution-credentials
On Unix guests, session programs execute with resolved passwd identities: uid,
gid and supplementary groups MUST be fully applied and every transition checked
before exec, and the exec environment MUST set TERM from the creating client,
COLORTERM=truecolor and a UTF-8 locale. Group membership is resolved before
fork. On Windows guests the `windows-terminal-session-identity` and
`windows-terminal-roster-accounts` contracts in `src/pty_windows.rs` apply
instead (ADR-0171).

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:architecture] one-session-engine
Linux, Windows, FreeBSD and illumos builds MUST share one session engine, framing, ring and HELLO
implementation; operating-system differences live only in the `conn`, `pty`,
`vsock` and boot-identity modules selected by target, so the wire protocol and
the capacity, fidelity and recovery contracts above hold on every supported target. Native linking MUST NOT establish kernel transport or
image admission (ADR-0190).

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:verification] terminald-hegel-properties
Hegel properties MUST cover, against independent oracles or round-trips: the
scrollback ring byte history, cursor gaps, configured-capacity boundaries,
shrink/grow and bounded overwrite backing; control frame encode/decode,
arbitrary chunking and header refusals; the HELLO, CREATE, KILL, RESIZE and
ATTACH payload codecs and their exact extents; split-invariant SHA-256/HMAC,
RFC 2104 long keys and constant-time comparison; the HELLO proof binding to
nonce, placement and epoch; the boot-identity lattice; JSON round-trip of
encoded log lines; and MMDS HTTP response framing. Real local PTY properties
MUST cover shared ring admission and retirement, actual growth backing,
mixed-pressure growth, natural-EOF release with a stalled tail, neighbor
progress beside a credit-stalled session and explicit kill of a credit-stalled
session. None of these establishes vsock, VM or guest isolation.
